Chapter: SmpAffinity

 A process pinned to a core runs on that core and on no other.

 process-spawn-on-core hands the scheduler a child together with the core it
 must run on. From then on only that core's idle-dispatch, preempt scan or
 yield will claim it: every one of those paths skips a candidate whose
 affinity is neither -1 (any core) nor the id of the core doing the claiming.

 The child is pinned to core 2 and asked which core actually ran it. A core
 stamps its own id into the process it claims, so the child reads that stamp
 back out of its own table entry -- process-get-pid gives its slot, and the
 core id lives eight bytes into the entry. If affinity holds, the only core
 that could have claimed a child pinned to 2 is core 2, so the stamp reads 2.

 The test can fail, which is the point. If the pin were ignored and the boot
 processor claimed the child, the stamp would read 0 and the test would say
 AFFINITY VIOLATED. If a pinned child were never claimable at all, the wait
 would never return and the test would time out. Reporting the core the child
 actually ran on, rather than that it merely finished, is what distinguishes
 a respected pin from a child that happened to run somewhere.

Section: Body

  spin : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (n) (acc) = if n == 0 then acc else spin (n - 1) (acc + 1)

  child-body : Integer -> Integer
  child-body (x) =
    let slot = process-get-pid
    in let core = atomic-load (20480 + slot * 256 + 8)
    in let w = atomic-store 36232 core
    in spin 2000000 0

  opening : [Console, Concurrent] Nothing = act
    a <- process-spawn-on-core (\x -> child-body x) 2
    wa <- process-wait a
    print-line-uni ("pinned-to-2 child ran on core " & show (atomic-load 36232))
    print-line-uni (if atomic-load 36232 == 2
                    then "affinity respected"
                    else "AFFINITY VIOLATED")
  end
